.SS "epx custom\-entities instances create"
Create an instance of a custom entity type.
.PP
.RS "4"
.nf
epx custom\-entities instances create CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID [flags]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Creates an instance of the custom entity type CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID from the JSON object in \fB\-\-file\fR, such as \fB{"id": "123", "name": {"en": "Report"}, "mixins": {\[u2026]}}\fR, and prints the id that Emporix answers with; it makes one unless the object names it, and an id that exists already is a conflict. An owner, such as \fB{"type": "EMPLOYEE", "userId": "\[u2026]"}\fR, cannot change once set. The attributes of a schema go under its id, which metadata.mixins maps to its URL: \fB{"mixins": {"SCHEMA_ID": {\[u2026]}}, "metadata": {"mixins": {"SCHEMA_ID": "URL"}}}\fR, the URL being the metadata.url that \fBepx schemas get SCHEMA_ID \-o json\fR prints. A localized attribute, unlike the name, is a list of translations, such as \fB[{"language": "en", "value": "Report"}]\fR. CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID is the ID that \fBepx custom\-entities list\fR shows.
.PP
Emporix checks the items that the references of the instance name (unless \fB\-\-validate\-references\fR false) with epx\(aqs token, which reads the instances of CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID alone: a reference to an instance of another custom entity type is then expected to fail with 400 Bad Request, as one to the type fails without that scope, and one to another kind of item, such as a product, may fail too.
.PP
Localized text in the body is a map of translations, e.g. \fB{"en": "Acme", "de": "Acme"}\fR, as \fB\-o json\fR of a read prints it, sent with Content\-Language: *. Emporix refuses a string in its place.
.PP
Changes data: on a terminal, epx asks before it sends POST. \fB\-\-yes\fR sends without asking (required without a terminal, or with \fB\-\-file\fR \-); \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR prints the request instead of sending it.
.PP
Where \fB\-\-validate\-references\fR is true or not given, epx also requests the custom.<type>_read scope.
.PP
Requires the custom.<type>_manage scope. <type> is the custom entity type in lower case (custom.document_manage for DOCUMENT).
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print the request instead of sending it (excludes \fB\-o\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-file <PATH>\fR
JSON request body in PATH, sent byte for byte (\- reads standard input; sending it then needs \fB\-\-yes\fR; required).
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output <format>\fR
Format of the response: json or yaml. Default: \fBjson\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-validate\-references <string>\fR
Whether Emporix checks that the items that references in the body name exist (true without it), requesting custom.<type>_read where it is true or not given: true or false.
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Send without asking (required without a terminal, or with \fB\-\-file\fR \-).
.SS "epx custom\-entities instances delete"
Delete an instance of a custom entity type.
.PP
.RS "4"
.nf
epx custom\-entities instances delete CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID ID [flags]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Deletes the instance ID of the custom entity type CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID; Emporix answers an unknown ID as deleted, not as not found. CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID is the ID that \fBepx custom\-entities list\fR shows, and ID the one that \fBepx custom\-entities instances list CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID\fR shows.
.PP
Deletes data, which cannot be undone. On a terminal, epx sends DELETE only once you type ID at its prompt. \fB\-\-yes\fR sends without asking (required without a terminal); \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR prints the request instead of sending it.
.PP
Requires the custom.<type>_manage scope. <type> is the custom entity type in lower case (custom.document_manage for DOCUMENT).
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print the request instead of sending it (excludes \fB\-o\fR).
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output <format>\fR
Format of the response: json or yaml. Default: \fBjson\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Send without asking (required without a terminal).
.SS "epx custom\-entities instances get"
Show one instance of a custom entity type.
.PP
.RS "4"
.nf
epx custom\-entities instances get CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID ID [flags]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads one instance of a custom entity type. CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID is the ID that \fBepx custom\-entities list\fR shows; an unknown one has no scope that Emporix grants (exit code 3), and a type created in the last minutes has none yet. ID is the ID that \fBepx custom\-entities instances list CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID\fR shows; an unknown one is not found. OWNER is an instance\(aqs owner, by default who created it (EMPLOYEE, CUSTOMER or SERVICE, and the user or client id). epx requests the scope of the type, not the tenant\-wide schema.custominstance_read that Emporix also accepts.
.PP
Where a mixin\(aqs schema is site\-aware, an item may hold, besides the mixin NAME, an overlay for a site, NAME__SITE, with the values that differ there. \fB\-\-mixin\-site\fR SITE sends SITE as the emporix\-site header: Emporix then merges the overlay of SITE into NAME (nested objects member by member; arrays and other values are replaced, not merged; a null leaves the base value) and leaves out every overlay. A site without an overlay, or one the tenant lacks (codes are case\-sensitive; epx does not check them), gets NAME as stored. Without \fB\-\-mixin\-site\fR, mixins and overlays are read as stored, whatever the default site of the profile. Tables leave mixins (attribute values) out; \fB\-o json\fR and \fB\-o yaml\fR print them, as \fB\-\-stream\fR does on a list.
.PP
A merged item is that site\(aqs view, not the item as stored. Do not write it back with update, bulk\-update or (products) import, which replace whole items and would delete every overlay, nor build a patch from it: NAME would take the site\(aqs values.
.PP
Requires the custom.<type>_read scope. <type> is the custom entity type in lower case (custom.document_read for DOCUMENT).
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-columns <names>\fR
Print only these columns of table and csv, in this order: comma\-separated names (any case) among ID, NAME, OWNER, VERSION.
.TP
\fB\-\-language <language>\fR
Language of localized table text; a missing translation shows another, labeled (\fB\-o json\fR prints all). Default: \fBen\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mixin\-site <SITE>\fR
Merge the site\-aware mixin overlays of the site SITE (NAME__SITE) into their mixins.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-headers\fR
Print table and csv without the header row.
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output <format>\fR
Output format: table, json, yaml or csv (csv: the table\(aqs rows; json and yaml: the whole response). Default: \fBtable\fR.
.SS "epx custom\-entities instances list"
List the instances of a custom entity type.
.PP
.RS "4"
.nf
epx custom\-entities instances list CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID [flags]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads the instances of a custom entity type (\fBepx custom\-entities instances get\fR shows one). CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID is the ID that \fBepx custom\-entities list\fR shows; an unknown one has no scope that Emporix grants (exit code 3), and a type created in the last minutes has none yet. OWNER is an instance\(aqs owner, by default who created it (EMPLOYEE, CUSTOMER or SERVICE, and the user or client id). epx requests the scope of the type, not the tenant\-wide schema.custominstance_read that Emporix also accepts.
.PP
Where a mixin\(aqs schema is site\-aware, an item may hold, besides the mixin NAME, an overlay for a site, NAME__SITE, with the values that differ there. \fB\-\-mixin\-site\fR SITE sends SITE as the emporix\-site header: Emporix then merges the overlay of SITE into NAME (nested objects member by member; arrays and other values are replaced, not merged; a null leaves the base value) and leaves out every overlay. A site without an overlay, or one the tenant lacks (codes are case\-sensitive; epx does not check them), gets NAME as stored. Without \fB\-\-mixin\-site\fR, mixins and overlays are read as stored, whatever the default site of the profile. Tables leave mixins (attribute values) out; \fB\-o json\fR and \fB\-o yaml\fR print them, as \fB\-\-stream\fR does on a list.
.PP
A merged item is that site\(aqs view, not the item as stored. Do not write it back with update, bulk\-update or (products) import, which replace whole items and would delete every overlay, nor build a patch from it: NAME would take the site\(aqs values.
.PP
Requires the custom.<type>_read scope. <type> is the custom entity type in lower case (custom.document_read for DOCUMENT).
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-columns <names>\fR
Print only these columns of table and csv, in this order: comma\-separated names (any case) among ID, NAME, OWNER, VERSION.
.TP
\fB\-\-language <language>\fR
Language of localized table text; a missing translation shows another, labeled (\fB\-o json\fR prints all). Default: \fBen\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-limit <n>\fR
Print at most n items; paged reads stop early where they safely can (0: every item).
.TP
\fB\-\-mixin\-site <SITE>\fR
Merge the site\-aware mixin overlays of the site SITE (NAME__SITE) into their mixins.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-headers\fR
Print table and csv without the header row.
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output <format>\fR
Output format: table, json, yaml or csv (csv: the table\(aqs rows; json and yaml: the whole response). Default: \fBtable\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-query <filter>\fR
Emporix q filter, passed as given, e.g. code:(A1,A2); space\-separated conditions must all match (fields mostly as in \fB\-o json\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-sort <order>\fR
Emporix sort order, passed as given, e.g. code or name.en:desc,code; fields mostly as in \fB\-o json\fR (Emporix may ignore an unknown one).
.TP
\fB\-\-stream\fR
Print items as JSON Lines as soon as they are checked, page by page on paged lists, reading up to 10000 pages where a list without it stops after 1000: a check failing on a later page exits 1 after the pages before it (excludes \fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-columns\fR and \fB\-\-no\-headers\fR).
.SS "epx custom\-entities instances patch"
Change an instance of a custom entity type.
.PP
.RS "4"
.nf
epx custom\-entities instances patch CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID ID [flags]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Changes the instance ID of the custom entity type CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID with the JSON array of JSON Patch (RFC 6902) operations in \fB\-\-file\fR, such as \fB[{"op": "replace", "path": "/name", "value": {"en": "Report"}}]\fR; an add whose path ends in /\- appends to an array. An unknown ID is not found. CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID is the ID that \fBepx custom\-entities list\fR shows, and ID the one that \fBepx custom\-entities instances list CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID\fR shows.
.PP
Emporix checks the items that the references of the instance name (unless \fB\-\-validate\-references\fR false) with epx\(aqs token, which reads the instances of CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID alone: a reference to an instance of another custom entity type is then expected to fail with 400 Bad Request, as one to the type fails without that scope, and one to another kind of item, such as a product, may fail too.
.PP
Localized text in the body is a map of translations, e.g. \fB{"en": "Acme", "de": "Acme"}\fR, as \fB\-o json\fR of a read prints it, sent with Content\-Language: *. Emporix refuses a string in its place.
.PP
Changes data: on a terminal, epx asks before it sends PATCH. \fB\-\-yes\fR sends without asking (required without a terminal, or with \fB\-\-file\fR \-); \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR prints the request instead of sending it.
.PP
Where \fB\-\-validate\-references\fR is true or not given, epx also requests the custom.<type>_read scope.
.PP
Requires the custom.<type>_manage scope. <type> is the custom entity type in lower case (custom.document_manage for DOCUMENT).
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print the request instead of sending it (excludes \fB\-o\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-file <PATH>\fR
JSON request body in PATH, sent byte for byte (\- reads standard input; sending it then needs \fB\-\-yes\fR; required).
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output <format>\fR
Format of the response: json or yaml. Default: \fBjson\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-validate\-references <string>\fR
Whether Emporix checks that the items that references in the body name exist (true without it), requesting custom.<type>_read where it is true or not given: true or false.
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Send without asking (required without a terminal, or with \fB\-\-file\fR \-).
.SS "epx custom\-entities instances search"
