.PP
.RS "4"
.nf
epx custom\-entities instances update CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID ID [flags]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Replaces the instance ID of the custom entity type CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID with the JSON object in \fB\-\-file\fR, as create takes it without the id, or creates it with that id where none exists (an upsert). With metadata.version, as \fBepx custom\-entities instances get CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID ID \-o json\fR prints it, Emporix replaces only that version: an older one is a conflict. CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID is the ID that \fBepx custom\-entities list\fR shows, and ID the one that \fBepx custom\-entities instances list CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID\fR shows.
.PP
Emporix checks the items that the references of the instance name (unless \fB\-\-validate\-references\fR false) with epx\(aqs token, which reads the instances of CUSTOM_ENTITY_ID alone: a reference to an instance of another custom entity type is then expected to fail with 400 Bad Request, as one to the type fails without that scope, and one to another kind of item, such as a product, may fail too.
.PP
Localized text in the body is a map of translations, e.g. \fB{"en": "Acme", "de": "Acme"}\fR, as \fB\-o json\fR of a read prints it, sent with Content\-Language: *. Emporix refuses a string in its place.
.PP
Changes data: on a terminal, epx asks before it sends PUT. \fB\-\-yes\fR sends without asking (required without a terminal, or with \fB\-\-file\fR \-); \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR prints the request instead of sending it.
.PP
Where \fB\-\-validate\-references\fR is true or not given, epx also requests the custom.<type>_read scope.
